1.Create a Google Cloud Project:If you don't already have one for this app.Go to the Google Cloud Console.Click the project dropdown at the top left (next to the Google Cloud logo) and select New Project.Name your project (e.g., your app's name) and click Create.2.Configure the OAuth Consent Screen:This defines what users see when they log in.In the left sidebar, navigate to APIs & Services > OAuth consent screen.Choose External (unless your app is strictly for internal company users) and click Create.Fill in the required fields: App name, user support email, and developer contact information.Under Authorized domains, add your production domain.Save and continue. You do not need to add sensitive scopes unless you are reading users' private Gmail or Drive files (basic login only requires openid, email, and profile).3.Create the OAuth 2.0 Client ID:In the left sidebar, click Credentials.Click the + CREATE CREDENTIALS button at the top and select OAuth client ID.Select Web application as the Application type.Name the client (e.g., "Laravel Web App").Under Authorized JavaScript origins, add your exact app URL (e.g., [https://your-api-domain.org](https://your-api-domain.org) or http://localhost:8000 for local development).Under Authorized redirect URIs, add the exact callback URL your app uses (e.g., [https://your-api-domain.org/api/v1/auth/google/callback](https://your-api-domain.org/api/v1/auth/google/callback)).Click Create.4.Update your .env file:Google will now display a modal window containing your Client ID and Client Secret.Open the .env file in the root directory of your Laravel project and add or update these lines:envGOOGLE_CLIENT_ID="your-long-client-id-string.apps.googleusercontent.com"
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET="your-secret-string"
OAUTH_DEV_BYPASS=false